2186415978 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534250 17592186045417 true] [17592186415978 1077 "selection" 13194139534250 17592186045417 true] [17592186415978 1078 #edb/ref 17592186415944 13194139534250 17592186045417 true] [17592186415978 1079 #uuid "d906cb4e-f500-54c4-87ea-509d8da51301" 13194139534250 17592186045417 true] [17592186415979 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415975 13194139534250 17592186045417 true] [17592186415979 1069 "q-161326" 13194139534250 17592186045417 true] [17592186415979 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534250 17592186045417 true] [17592186415979 1071 "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{2}y,$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=-\\sqrt{2}x-y$ for $-1\\le x\\le 0$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" 13194139534250 17592186045417 true] [17592186415979 1072 #uuid "3e3b0ae8-2464-5f3a-ba5b-28ac6b071959" 13194139534250 17592186045417 true] [17592186415979 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-\\sqrt{2}x-y)=-\\sqrt{2},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\sqrt{2}x-y)=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(-\\sqrt{2})}^{2}+{(-1)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\sqrt{2}y\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,2\\sqrt{2}y\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534250 17592186045417 true] [17592186415980 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415977 13194139534250 17592186045417 true] [17592186415980 1069 "q-161342" 13194139534250 17592186045417 true] [17592186415980 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534250 17592186045417 true] [17592186415980 1071 "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}2z\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=x$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" 13194139534250 17592186045417 true] [17592186415980 1072 #uuid "44573c5a-fe32-5ee3-9d3f-c65bffe1a9fd" 13194139534250 17592186045417 true] [17592186415980 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}2z\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\sqrt{1+{1}^{2}+(0)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{∬}_{D}\\,2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y]_{y=0}^{y=\\pi /2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}-2x(0-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{[{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534250 17592186045417 true] [17592186415981 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415973 13194139534250 17592186045417 true] [17592186415981 1069 "q-161351" 13194139534250 17592186045417 true] [17592186415981 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534250 17592186045417 true] [17592186415981 1071 "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}(24xy+{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=3x-4y$ for $1\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$" 13194139534250 17592186045417 true] [17592186415981 1072 #uuid "4f6d27cd-0aea-5d32-8f1f-4a62535f9e3b" 13194139534250 17592186045417 true] [17592186415981 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x-4y)=3,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x-4y)=-4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}(24xy+{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(24xy+(3x-4y)^{2})\\sqrt{1+{3}^{2}+(-4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{26}{∬}_{D}\\,(24xy+9{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}-24xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{26}{∬}_{D}\\,(9{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{26}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{26}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{[9{x}^{2}y+\\frac{16}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{26}{\\int }_{1}^{2}(9{x}^{2}+\\frac{16}{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{26}{[3{x}^{3}+\\frac{16}{3}x]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{26}(24+\\frac{32}{3}-3-\\frac{16}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{79}{3}\\sqrt{26}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534250 17592186045417 true] [17592186415982 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415974 13194139534250 17592186045417 true] [17592186415982 1069 "q-161316" 13194139534250 17592186045417 true] [17592186415982 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534250 17592186045417 true] [17592186415982 1071 "![](images/0b/0b1b263baf01b692a0344388207c12d3c4dac0f49d11cd4bfa744f2841df2c6b.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\frac{4xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=x-\\sqrt{2}y$ and whose domain is the quarter-circle of radius $3$ centered at $O$ in the fourth quadrant, as shown above." 13194139534250 17592186045417 true] [17592186415982 1072 #uuid "9c04d8b1-030c-5d18-9c3c-13b32378d108" 13194139534250 17592186045417 true] [17592186415982 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x-\\sqrt{2}y)=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x-\\sqrt{2}y)=-\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\frac{4xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{4xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{1}^{2}+(-\\sqrt{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{4xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{4}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{4xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{4xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =2{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}\\frac{4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot r\\text{ }\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}\\frac{4{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{3}2r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta [{r}^{2}]_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =9[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ]_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =9\\cdot (-1-1) \\\\  & =-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534250 17592186045417 true] [17592186415983 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415976 13194139534250 17592186045417 true] [17592186415983 1069 "q-161338" 13194139534250 17592186045417 true] [17592186415983 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534250 17592186045417 true] [17592186415983 1071 "![](images/fe/fe04241f55bae9dae231be6486a3c53985c8dc4af9e9656787267d9d852f829e.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}(1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=4x+\\sqrt{3}y$ and whose domain is the circle of radius $1$ centered at $O,$ as shown above." 13194139534250 17592186045417 true] [17592186415983 1072 #uuid "ac50dc50-55cb-5157-8205-32f49dbad931" 13194139534250 17592186045417 true] [17592186415983 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x+\\sqrt{3}y)=4,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x+\\sqrt{3}y)=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}(1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{4}^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\cdot \\sqrt{20}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}{∬}_{D}\\,(1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{5}{∬}_{D}\\,(1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & =2\\sqrt{5}{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}{∬}_{Δ}(1-{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}(r-{r}^{3})\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534250 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-008daf1a-a433-5118-aad3-61bc2cd82151" 17592186415943 "answer-0be6bfb1-22ec-54d7-8d70-408044da5849" 17592186415944 "answer-11622f2c-a064-5989-bfd9-037259b1990a" 17592186415945 "answer-11f496da-87e3-5a3b-81b0-ad4ccd99eec9" 17592186415946 "answer-137ced3c-e27e-55d0-a129-d7f6d14ddfc4" 17592186415947 "answer-193323db-4837-5978-b14e-f2ac7216eb5b" 17592186415948 "answer-1ac43cfe-450e-5f00-a858-df79110cc719" 17592186415949 "answer-1dee489f-a3c9-5e67-9010-8a2bb6344bd1" 17592186415950 "answer-20eaebd4-47b4-5a06-b816-cc531888309e" 17592186415951 "answer-246